# Hostile audit — collapsed anchor destination capacity

The capacity argument uses only strict convexity of bounded activated contact faces.
At most two side vectors on such a face can lie on one projective line: three would
force two with the same orientation, while opposite orientations can occur only as a
single pair in the cyclic boundary of a strictly convex polygon.

The actual outer face is excluded from the capacity count, so repeated outer-walk
occurrences and bridges are not silently ruled out.  The resulting inequality
`6m6 <= r_O + 2a_F` is therefore safe under the current occurrence-counted outer
boundary conventions.

The reduction does not say that different collapsed components have disjoint
activated destinations, nor that a common outer host has simple sectors.  Those are
the remaining global scope conditions.

**Audit disposition:** PASS at the stated finite capacity scope; no canonical state
change and no C-II closure.
